2026-06-30 14:54:15,788 - openai._base_client - DEBUG - Request options: {'method': 'post', 'url': '/chat/completions', 'timeout': 300, 'files': None, 'idempotency_key': 'stainless-python-retry-2fe82793-56bc-4be3-815c-911019c77f9d', 'security': {'bearer_auth': True}, 'content': None, 'json_data': {'messages': [{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You are an expert SDRF-Proteomics annotator for crosslinking mass spectrometry (XL-MS) datasets.\n\nExtract the following SDRF factor value terms. Factor values identify which metadata\nattribute is the primary experimental variable being compared in the study.\n\nTERMS TO EXTRACT:\n─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nterm: factor value cell line\nsdrf_column: factor value[cell line]\nontology: CLO:0000031\ndescription: Indicates cell line is the primary experimental variable.\ninstructions: Return a value if different cell lines are compared across conditions.\n  Otherwise return null.\n\nterm: factor value compound\nsdrf_column: factor value[compound]\nontology: CHEBI:37577\ndescription: Indicates compound/drug treatment is the primary experimental variable.\ninstructions: Return the compound name if different compounds or drug doses are\n  compared. Otherwise return null.\n\nterm: factor value disease\nsdrf_column: factor value[disease]\nontology: OGMS:0000031, MONDO, EFO, DOID\ndescription: Indicates disease state is the primary experimental variable.\ninstructions: Return disease name/accession if diseased vs. normal or different\n  disease states are compared. Otherwise return null.\n\nterm: factor value dose\nsdrf_column: factor value[dose]\nontology: EFO:0000428\ndescription: Indicates dose/concentration is the primary experimental variable.\npattern: number + unit\ninstructions: Return the dose range if different doses of a compound are compared.\n  Otherwise return null.\n\nterm: factor value organism part\nsdrf_column: factor value[organism part]\nontology: EFO:0000635, UBERON, BTO\ndescription: Indicates tissue/organ is the primary experimental variable.\ninstructions: Return the tissue/organ name if different tissues are compared.\n  Otherwise return null.\n\nterm: factor value time\nsdrf_column: factor value[time]\nontology: EFO:0000721\ndescription: Indicates time point is the primary experimental variable.\npattern: number + time unit\ninstructions: Return the time points compared if a time-course experiment.\n  Otherwise return null.\n\nterm: factor value treatment\nsdrf_column: factor value[treatment]\nontology: EFO:0000727, NCIT, EFO\ndescription: Indicates treatment/perturbation is the primary experimental variable.\ninstructions: Return the treatment type if different treatments are compared\n  (e.g. different growth conditions, carbon sources, stress conditions).\n  Otherwise return null.\n─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\nReturn a single JSON object where each key is the exact sdrf_column string shown above.\nFor each key, return a dict with:\n  "value": the extracted value (string or null — null if this variable is not\n           the primary experimental factor in this study)\n  "accession": ontology accession if resolvable, else null\n  "confidence": "high", "medium", or "low"\n  "evidence_quote": short verbatim quote from the text, or null\n  "evidence_location": location in the paper (e.g. "Abstract", "Methods"), or null\n\nNote: Most XL-MS datasets will have only one or two non-null factor values.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': '[TITLE]\nComplementary Benzophenone Cross-Linking/Mass Spectrometry Photochemistry\n\n[ABSTRACT]\nUse of a heterobifunctional photoactivatable cross-linker, sulfo-SDA (diazirine), has yielded high-density data that facilitated structure modeling of individual proteins. We expand the photoactivatable chemistry toolbox here with a second reagent, sulfo-SBP (benzophenone). This further increases the density of photo-cross-linking to a factor of 20× over conventional cross-linking. Importantly, the two different photoactivatable groups display orthogonal directionality, enabling access to different protein regions, unreachable with a single cross-linker.\n\n[METHODS]\nMethods\nChemicals and Materials\nThe cross-linking reagent sulfo-SDA was purchased from Thermo Fisher Scientific (Rockford, IL). HSA (A8763) was purchased from Sigma-Aldrich (St. Louis, MO).\nSynthesis of 4-(N-Sulfosuccinimidylcarboxy)benzophenone\n4-Benzoylbenzoic acid (37 mg, 0.16 mmol) and N-hydroxysulfosuccinimide (50 mg, 0.23 mmol) were stirred in 1 mL of DMF and to this was added EDC·HCl (40 mg, 0.21 mmol) and the mixture stirred overnight. Solvents were removed under reduced pressure and the residue purified using semipreparative RP-HPLC (Agilent 1100 prep-HPLC system equipped with a preparative autosampler (G2260A), preparative scale pumps (G1361A), a fraction collector (G1364B-prep), and a multiwavelength UV detector (G13658 MWD with preparative flow cell)). Material was separated at a flow rate of 20 mL/min on an Agilent RP-C-18 column (21.2 mm × 150 mm, 10 μm particle size), using the following method: 0% B for 5 min, 0% to 80% B in 40 min, 80% B for 5 min, 80% to 0% B in 5 min, 0% B for 5 min, with mobile phase A consisting of water and 0.1% trifluoroacetic acid and mobile phase B consisting of acetonitrile and 0.1% trifluoroacetic acid) to yield the title product as a white solid (20 mg, 0.05 mmol, 31%). 1H-NMR (500 MHz, D2O) δ 8.29–8.15 (2H, m, ArH), 7.92–7.66 (5H, m, ArH), 7.61–7.48 (2H, m, ArH), 4.55 (1H, d, J = 7.1 H, SCH), 3.46 (1H, dd, J = 18.0, 8.7 Hz, CH2), 3.26 (1H, m, CH2). 13C-NMR (125 MHz, D2O) δ 199.2 (C), 170.0 (C), 166.0 (C), 161.9 (C), 142.6 (C), 135.8 (C), 134.1 (CH), 130.5 (CH), 130.4 (CH), 130.3 (CH), 128.7 (CH), 127.6 (C), 56.6 (CH), 30.0 (CH2). MS (ESI) Exact mass calcd for C18H14NO8S [M + H]+, 404.44; found, 404.15.\nCross-Linking HSA\nCross-linking was carried out in triplicate. Aliquots of HSA (15 μg, 0.75 mg/mL)